---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PlatformGraph from '../components/PlatformGraph.tsx';
import graph from '../data/graph.json';
import '../styles/graph.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const layers = graph.layers.map((l) => ({
  ...l,
  nodes: graph.nodes.filter((n) => n.layer === l.id),
}));
---

<Base
  title="How I work — Vitalii Ryzhenko"
  description="My agent platform: devices, private access, agents with an independent verifier, written rules, services and records."
  wide
>
  <h1>How I work</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    <strong>In short:</strong> AI agents do real work on my infrastructure — but only under written rules, with a second,
    independent agent checking every change, and designed so private data stays on the machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    I run my own projects the way I would want a team's agents to run: on written rules, with an independent check
    before and after every change, and with a record of what happened. Below is the platform as it is today —
    one home server, a private network and coding agents. The projects that run on it — jev, Interview Gym, the
    status board — have their own case studies.
  </p>

  <PlatformGraph client:visible />

  <ul class="pg-legend" aria-label="Legend">
    <li><span class="pg-dot pg-dot-running"></span>running</li>
    <li><span class="pg-dot pg-dot-code-only"></span>code only, not deployed</li>
    <li><span class="pg-dot pg-dot-active"></span>in use</li>
    <li>Teal — inside the private tailnet</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The loop behind every change</h2>
  <ol class="loop">
    <li><strong>Rules.</strong> Conventions, ten security policies, permissions and procedures live in a git wiki. Agents read them before acting.</li>
    <li><strong>Executor.</strong> <code>/make &lt;intent&gt;</code> finds the matching procedure, fills its inputs and writes a plan. It asks a human where the permissions say so.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent verifier.</strong> A separate agent with a fresh context, read-only by its brief, returns GO/STOP on the plan and PASS/FAIL from deterministic checks after the change.</li>
    <li><strong>Log.</strong> The session is written to a log; if reality differed from the procedure, the executor proposes a wiki diff for review.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="checking-ai-code">How I check AI-written code</h2>
  <p>
    Agents write most of the code in my projects. None of it reaches <code>main</code> on the author agent's word.
    The repositories named below are private, so you won't find links to them. The case studies describe them.
  </p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>
      <strong>An independent verifier before and after each <code>/make</code> step.</strong> A separate agent with its
      own context returns GO/STOP on the plan. After the change it runs the deterministic checks and returns PASS/FAIL.
      <span class="where">agent-wiki: <code>agents/agents/verifier.md</code>, <code>checks/</code> ·
      <a href={`${base}/work/platform/`}>platform case</a></span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A clean-context review before merge.</strong> The reviewer agent sees only the task, the diff and the
      check results. It never sees the author's reasoning. On one PR (agent-wiki PR #13) it found 10 defects before merge.
      <span class="where">agent-wiki: <code>agents/skills/second-opinion</code> · as of 2026-11-06</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One <code>verify.sh</code> per project: lint, tests and gitleaks.</strong> Exit code 3 means "not
      verified": a skipped step blocks the change just like a failure. Nothing turns green without running.
      <span class="where">babyloop, agent-memory: <code>scripts/verify.sh</code> ·
      <a href={`${base}/work/babyloop/`}>babyloop case</a></span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Only two human gates: spec and merge.</strong> Everything in between is automated and logged. At the end
      of a session a hook writes the Session Log entry from git and GitHub.
      <span class="where">agent-wiki: <code>hooks/session-end.py</code></span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Prompt evals at work.</strong> At Creatio I ran evals on more than 50 production prompts and cut their
      token use by 40%.
      <span class="where">as of 2026-11-06</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <noscript>
    <h2>Platform, as text</h2>
  </noscript>
  <details class="as-text">
    <summary>The same graph as a list</summary>
    {
      layers.map((l) => (
        <section>
          <h3>{l.label}</h3>
          <ul>
            {l.nodes.map((n) => (
              <li>
                <strong>{n.label}</strong> — {n.card.what} <em>Decision:</em> {n.card.decision}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))
    }
  </details>

  <p class="muted source">
    Generated from the service registry (wiki commit <code>{graph.source.commit}</code>) through a public allowlist:
    only status and whether a service is inside the private network are taken from the registry; hostnames, addresses
    and ports never reach this page.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .loop li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .checks li {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .checks .where {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .as-text {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .as-text summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .source {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
</style>
